# Notofilia — No Duplicate Banknotes

Every banknote in the Notofilia catalogue is a physical, unique object. The serial number is its identity. **Never add a banknote whose serial number already exists in the catalogue.** Checking is mandatory, not optional, and happens *before* any file is created or edited.

This rule applies to every feature branch and Cursor cloud agent working on Notofilia.com. Catalogue data lives in `src/data/` (country modules and `*.ts` piece records), generated images under `public/images/catalog/` (uncommitted), and masters under `catalog-src/catalog/`. There is no `src/content/` collection yet; still search it if it appears.

## 1. Before adding any banknote

Run these checks in order and report the results in your response before writing anything.

### 1.1 Normalize the serial number

Serials arrive in inconsistent forms (`A 1234567`, `A-1234567`, `a1234567`, `Nº 1234567`). Normalize before comparing:

- Uppercase everything.
- Strip spaces, hyphens, dots, `Nº`, `No.`, `#`, and other punctuation.
- Keep prefix letters, digits, and suffix letters together (`A1234567B`).
- Do **not** strip leading zeros (`0012345` ≠ `12345`).
- If the note has two serials (left/right, or prefix + counter), record both, and compare both.

Compare only normalized-to-normalized.

### 1.2 Search the whole catalogue for the serial

Search every place a serial can live — content collections, data files, and image filenames — in both the Spanish root tree and the `/en/` tree:

```bash
# exact normalized serial (adjust the pattern to the normalized value)
rg -i --no-heading "A1234567" src/ public/ docs/ catalog-src/

# loose match: same digits with any separators between characters
rg -i --no-heading "A[ .-]?1[ .-]?2[ .-]?3[ .-]?4[ .-]?5[ .-]?6[ .-]?7" src/ public/ docs/ catalog-src/
```

Also grep the frontmatter/data field directly if the collection has one:

```bash
rg -i "serial(_number|_display)?:\s*.*1234567" src/content/ src/data/
```

A hit in **any** file — Spanish page, English page, JSON/YAML/TS data, image name, draft, or archived entry — means the note already exists. Stop and go to §3.

Hits only in editorial copy (`src/data/blog-articles.json`, `src/data/news-articles.json`) are not catalogue holdings unless they cite a collection serial that already lives in a country module. Hits only in `docs/sources/heritage/` are Heritage auction lots (comparables), not Notofilia holdings — even when Pick and date match a collection type. Treat a match in a country module (`src/data/*.ts`) as a definite duplicate.

### 1.3 Secondary fingerprint check (serial unreadable or missing)

If the serial is illegible, absent (proofs, specimens, essays, some 19th‑century private-bank issues), or the user could not supply it, fall back to the combined fingerprint. Two entries match if **all** of these agree:

- Issuer / bank (e.g. Banco de la República, Banco de Río Hacha)
- Denomination and currency
- Date printed on the note (exact date, not just year, when present)
- Pick / catalogue number (Pick, PMG/NGC cert number, or Notofilia internal ID)
- Signature combination
- Certification number (PMG / NGC / PCGS) when the note is graded — a cert number is as unique as a serial; treat a cert-number match as a definite duplicate

When you must rely on the fingerprint, say so explicitly in your response and ask the user to confirm the serial or cert number before creating the entry.

### 1.4 Check the bilingual pair

Every catalogue item has a Spanish page and an English page (hreflang pair). A duplicate check that only searches one language tree is incomplete. If the serial exists in one language only, that is a **missing translation**, not a new note — add the missing pair, don't create a second item.

This site stores one data record per piece in `src/data/` and thin ES/EN routes that share a `*Page.astro` type layout. A missing pair is a missing route or `PATH_PREFIX_PAIRS` entry, not a second object in the data module.

## 2. When creating the entry

- Store the serial in a dedicated frontmatter/data field (`serial`), in normalized form, plus `serial_display` for the exact printed form if they differ.
- Store `cert_number` and `cert_grader` for graded notes.
- Give the item one stable internal ID shared by both language pages, so the dedup check can key on it later.
- Name image files with the internal ID or normalized serial, never with a generic name (`billete-5-pesos.jpg` invites duplicates; `NF-0421-A1234567-front.jpg` does not). Masters go in `catalog-src/catalog/` using the public path pattern (`philippines/1-peso-front.jpg`).
- Finish your response with a one-line dedup statement, e.g.:
  `Dedup: searched src/, public/, docs/, catalog-src/ for A1234567 (and loose variants) — 0 matches. Cert 8071234-001 — 0 matches.`

## 3. When a duplicate is found

Do **not** create a new entry. Instead:

1. Show the user the existing file path(s) and the matching serial / cert / fingerprint.
2. Ask whether they want to (a) update the existing entry with the new information or images, (b) add the missing language pair, or (c) confirm it is a different note (e.g. a re-used serial across series — rare, but possible; require the user's explicit confirmation and record why in the entry).
3. Never silently overwrite, rename, or merge entries.

## 4. Bulk imports (CSV, spreadsheet, batch of images)

- First dedup the import against **itself** (same serial appearing twice in the batch).
- Then dedup the batch against the catalogue.
- Produce a table before writing anything: `serial | status (new / exists / missing pair / needs review) | existing path`.
- Only write the rows marked `new` and `missing pair`; hold everything else for the user.

## 5. Periodic audit

When asked to audit the catalogue, list any serial or cert number that appears in more than one item, any item missing a serial without a `no_serial_reason` field, and any bilingual pair where only one language exists. Report — don't fix — unless the user asks.
